## Lessons (most important first)
-16. (game 33) When two of my groups each drop to 2-3 libs at once (C3C4 and E3E4
   after White D4), the move that CONNECTS them (D3) beats any capture elsewhere.
   A 3-3 corner stone attached underneath (B3) needs B2+C2-type eye shape, not a
   C-column wall.
-15. (game 31) When the opponent presses my 3rd-line corner stone from above (W G5
   vs my G3), answer SOLIDLY next to my stone (G4 hane on top or H3 under), never a
   diagonal/knight jump (H4) that the opponent can cut through. Losing a corner =
   ~10 points on 9x9 = the game.
-14. (game 28) Before ANY hane, count the hane stone's libs after the opponent's
   simplest reply (extend/hane back). H5 and B6 in game 28 each became 1-lib
   stones immediately. If <=2 libs and a cut exists, play the solid move instead.
-13. (game 27) Before playing a dividing-line stone, check if it TOUCHES/diagonals
   enemy stones. If the opponent can wedge + cut and my stones end with 2 libs, play
   one line lower and connected (E4 not E5 vs row-6 wall). When a group of mine is
   cut and has 2 libs with the opponent to move, SACRIFICE it immediately; don't
   add stones.
-12. (game 21) An invasion group with <=3 libs must NOT cut or fight; play the
   corner/edge eye move (2nd line, e.g. H2/J3) first. Run libs.py at every move of
   an invasion — I didn't, and my group sat on 2-3 libs for 6 moves.
-11. (game 20) An invasion group that wins its fight still needs TWO EYES. Every
   "connecting" move inside its own space (G5, H5) destroys an eye. Before each
   move on such a group, list its empty interior points; if fewer than ~6 or
   they form a line, play the edge/eye move (H9/J8 corner, 2nd-line) FIRST.
   1st-line placements next to my edge stones (black H9 next to H8) kill — take
   that point myself early.
-10. (game 19) A wall group (row 3) with my territory under it is NOT alive if the
   opponent has a stone touching that territory (D3). Count EYES of every group,
   not just libs: zero real eyes + opponent stones nearby = defend first.
-9. (game 18) After every edge block/hane (D9, E8 type), list my stones with <=2 libs
   and the empty cutting points next to them. A 1-lib stone on the edge + an empty
   diagonal cut = Black cuts with atari. Spend one move to make it solid when ahead.
-8. (game 16) SEKI = DEAD for this engine's scoring. Make two real eyes, or if the
   group can only make seki, keep playing (e.g. find other gains) — don't pass into it.
-7. (game 15) When the opponent invades UNDER my 4th-line stones (E3 between C4/G4),
   take away the base: play underneath/beside on rows 2-3 (D2, F2, or the knight-move
   undercut), not the cap on top. A capped 3rd-line stone just crawls and lives.
-6. (game 14) When I have two stones on one side (C3 + D7) and the opponent can play
   between them (C5), that split point is the most urgent point — take it or a move
   that makes it fail BEFORE extending elsewhere (E7 was too slow).
-5. (game 13) Before playing a cap/attachment next to enemy stones, check my new
   stone's libs after the opponent's obvious cut (G4 in game 13 had 1 lib after G5).
   Wedged stones between two enemy groups die; play solid (connect) instead.
-4. (games 5, 7) After every exchange ask: "who got the EDGE out of this?" If White
   gets row 1-2 and I get a row-4 wall, I lost ~2 points per stone exchanged.
   Blocking from the center side is wrong when the opponent is the one on the edge.
-3. (game 6) Invasion life: an L-shaped group on rows 7-8 needs F9-type edge moves
   AND the vital point of its eyespace. Bulky-five/pyramid shapes: occupy the vital
   point yourself. Before each life move, run libs.py on the board AFTER the move
   and after the opponent's most annoying reply (atari / throw-in).
